1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,375
Why Leaves Change Color.

2
00:00:02,325 --> 00:00:13,200
Every autumn, deciduous trees across temperate regions produce one of nature's most striking displays: the transformation of green foliage into brilliant shades of red, orange, and yellow.

3
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:22,225
This process, far from random, involves a carefully orchestrated series of biochemical events driven by decreasing day length and lower temperatures.

4
00:00:22,225 --> 00:00:35,150
During the growing season, leaves appear green because chloroplasts within leaf cells are packed with chlorophyll, the pigment that absorbs red and blue wavelengths from sunlight and converts them into chemical energy through photosynthesis.

5
00:00:35,150 --> 00:00:40,312
Chlorophyll concentrations are so high that they mask other pigments present in the leaf.

6
00:00:40,312 --> 00:00:46,325
As autumn approaches and daylight shortens, trees receive the signal to prepare for winter dormancy.

7
00:00:46,325 --> 00:00:53,700
They begin breaking down chlorophyll and reabsorbing its nitrogen and magnesium components for storage in branches and roots.

8
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:58,362
Once chlorophyll degrades, two other pigment classes become visible.

9
00:00:58,362 --> 00:01:04,700
Carotenoids—the same compounds that color carrots and corn—produce yellow and orange hues.

10
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:12,800
These fat-soluble pigments reside in the chloroplast membrane throughout the growing season but remain hidden behind chlorophyll's dominant green.

11
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:19,475
Unlike chlorophyll, carotenoids are relatively stable in cool temperatures and persist as the green fades.

12
00:01:19,475 --> 00:01:26,450
Red and purple colors result from anthocyanins, water-soluble pigments synthesized in vacuoles during autumn.

13
00:01:26,450 --> 00:01:32,375
Unlike carotenoids, anthocyanins are produced only in autumn, not present year-round.

14
00:01:32,375 --> 00:01:43,912
Research published by plant physiologists at the University of Vermont indicates that anthocyanins act as a sunscreen, protecting leaf cells from excess light during the chlorophyll-reabsorption process.

15
00:01:43,912 --> 00:01:52,362
High sugar content and bright light promote anthocyanin production, which explains why the most vivid reds occur during sunny, cool autumns.

16
00:01:52,362 --> 00:01:57,312
The timing and intensity of color change vary by species and geography.

17
00:01:57,312 --> 00:02:08,812
Sugar maples in New England, at elevations between 300 and 900 meters, produce some of the most intense displays when autumn temperatures range between 0 and 7 degrees Celsius.

18
00:02:08,812 --> 00:02:16,987
By understanding the pigment chemistry behind autumn color, scientists can also track shifts in seasonal timing linked to climate change.

